{"type":"text","timestamp":1792483508738,"sessionID":"ses_ea7ae49edffeqHl0eGUfeYjbPJ","part":{"id":"prt_15857ad51001PWg8gmt3MPtuyO","messageID":"msg_158577820001cdXHS9mA3wwjif","sessionID":"ses_ea7ae49edffeqHl0eGUfeYjbPJ","type":"text","text":"\n\nThe file is complete and verified. The implementation is a lock-free SPSC ring buffer for Zephyr:\n\n**`submission/main.c`** (self-contained, header + source + demo in one file):\n\n- **Header section** (Part 1): `ring_buf_t` plus `ring_buf_init/write/read/write_buf/read_buf/is_empty/is_full/count/free_space`.\n- **Core design** (Part 2): monotonic `head`/`tail` `atomic_t` counters — the producer (ISR) owns `head`, the consumer (thread) owns `tail`. Each side snapshots the other with `atomic_get()` and publishes its own update with `atomic_set()`, whose fence guarantees item data is visible before the index advertises it. No mutexes/spinlocks/critical sections anywhere.\n  - **Full**: producer refuses to write when `head - tail >= size` (`ring_buf_write` returns false / bulk returns count stored).\n  - **Empty**: consumer sees `head == tail` and returns false.\n  - **FIFO**: single reader/writer per end plus the `head - tail <= size` invariant guarantee ordering; monotonic counters avoid the wasted-slot trick, so all 32 slots are usable.\n- **Demo** (Part 3): a `k_timer` expiry callback (runs in ISR context, like a UART RX interrupt) produces 1 byte/5 ms while a cooperative thread consumes in 8-byte bursts/50 ms, so both the full-drop and empty paths are exercised; status is printed periodically with consumed/dropped counters.","time":{"start":1792483503441,"end":1792483508682}}}
